 To obtain our result, we introduce a new lower bound graph construction, as well as a new communication complexity problem that we call Overlap. As this problem does not appear to be amenable to reductions to existing hard problems such as set disjointness or indexing due to correlations between the inputs of the three players, we leverage results from cross-intersecting set families to prove the hardness of Overlap for deterministic algorithms in the 3-party model with simultaneous messages. Finally, we obtain the sought lower bound for deciding
 k
 -edge connectivity via a novel simulation argument that, in contrast to previous works, does not introduce any probability of error and thus works for deterministic algorithms.
